[Music]  welcome back to curious archive this  video is the second half of a series  exploring the biology and ecosystems of  realistic pokemon the realistic pokemon  art project was created by the talented  rj palmer aka arvalis and i've links  below where you can follow and support  his work this second round of redesigns  features amazing biological details and  an even wider variety of pokemon but  please remember the disclaimer from the  first video these realistic life forms  are an exciting speculative exercise but  not an attempt to somehow improve the  stylized designs of the original games  they're just fun alternate takes that  imagine pokemon in something closer to  real world anatomy although of course  these life forms are more fantastical  than what would likely appear in nature  with that said let's jump into part 2 of  our nature documentary of another world  and return to the astonishing domain of  realistic pokemon  beginning with gen 2 starters we have  meganium in its pre-evolutions the  designs of these creatures take  inspiration from sauropods but also from  modern tortoises and while tortoises  have evolved to eat grasses palmer  imagines the long necks of meganium help  them reach their preferred food of  treetop fungi and speaking of fungi the  antenna at the end of meganium's  nostrils are actually a unique strain of  fungi which palmer states the titans  have a symbiotic relationship with  although the exact purpose of this is  unknown the flower-like structure on the  front of their shell is not a plant  however but a brightly colored frill  that mimics flower petals this might  seem strange but animals camouflaging  themselves as plants has been documented  numerous times in nature like insects  whose wings have evolved to resemble  leaves  up next we have typhlosion and its  pre-evolutions reimagined with greater  detail and anatomical plausibility in  its claws and fur pattern this  reimagining resembles a cross between  some sort of badger and a sun bear  palmer imagines typhlosion feeds  primarily on comb bee hives and the wax  from the hives has a chemical reaction  with typhlosion saliva to create a  flammable substance typhlosions then use  their tongues to coat their quills in  this chemical and then light them on  fire using specialized flint-like teeth  the blazing quills are an intimidating  display and can burn for long periods  thanks to typhlosion's fire resistant  coat a really original explanation and  the last starter from the hoenn region  is for alligator and its pre-evolutions  rendered in all their reptilian glory  not surprisingly fair alligator is most  analogous to earth alligators or  crocodiles palmer imagines this life  form as a large opportunistic carnivore  lurking in coastal estuaries and  frequently traveling between salt and  freshwater habitats i'd probably watch  my step near rivers with these giants  around moving beyond generation 2 we  have the incredible desert dwelling  flygon palmer envisions that this swift  aerial lifeform possesses a helium  bladder that helps the large organism  achieve flight fly guns skim the surface  of the desert looking out from behind a  red transparent membrane which protects  their eyes from the sands and on the  sands near some other interesting desert  fauna i'll let you classify on your own  we can find a trap pinch and a vibrava  both reimagined to look like they could  more feasibly be early stages in a  flygon's life cycle  in this alternative world millions of  years ago large prehistoric pokemon  stomped across the land now known to  scientists in the form of fossils taking  inspiration from the games palmer  imagines recent technology has revived  these long extinct species like the  mighty tyrantum and the lumbering  bastiodon pictured here locked in combat  and poses reminiscent of classic  dinosaur art not surprisingly palmer has  based his tyrantum off the t-rex and has  imagined its beard as a scaly soft  tissue pouch that could flare out to  intimidate rivals it's exciting to  imagine these creatures living in the  primordial landscape of the pokemon  world 100 million years ago it almost  makes me want to visit a pokemon museum  of natural history another fossil  pokemon you might find at such a museum  is that of aerodactyl now resurrected as  living creatures the aerodactyl are now  known by pokemon scientists to be a  vibrant shade of purple and to possess  early protofeathers on their necks  elsewhere in this image one can also  spot an arc hen and a cranny dose  further fleshing out the world of  prehistoric pokemon  deep in the midst of a past ice age the  mighty legendary pokemon qrem reigned  supreme here palmer has redesigned the  stylized armor of the original as more  biological plating though kyurem was a  creature of almost insurmountable power  the legendary beast raiku imagined here  as a smilodon like ice age hunter would  sometimes take the risk and try to  ambush this titan though a bite of a  raiku could take out most pokemon palmer  imagines it had less effect on  piloswines depicted in this image's ice  age megafauna with coats so thick they  provide a natural defense from the teeth  of whatever else might be stalking the  tundra  in modern polar and mountainous biomes  one might find the elusive obama snow  portrayed as a curious heavily furred  biped that palmer imagines were unknown  to pokemon scientists until the turn of  the century when a specimen was at last  documented sharing the frigid biosphere  with these seldom glimpsed creatures are  the elk-like pokemon winter sauce box  and the penguin-like deli bird another  life-form in this image is the infamous  mr mime reimagined as a species  somewhere between a frog and an axolotl  and honestly i find this redesign a bit  nicer to look at than the original  lurking in the cave systems one can find  neuvern a species that is a dragon type  in the games but palmer has  reconceptualized as a creature more  closely related to bat pokemon like  swoobats a single neuvern can claim  miles of cave systems as their territory  scaring off competitors with booming  sonic frequencies swoo bats are their  smaller cousins and look much closer to  a traditional bat here on earth and if  you look closely you can see jolticks  clinging to the fur of these species  palmer imagines these small arachnid  pokemon can create static charges by  nestling into the fur of other fluffy  pokemon pretty creative  one massive pokemon palmer redesigned is  onyx and its evolution stelix instead of  being made of literal rock onyx is  depicted as a massive serpentine  lifeform with rock-like scales likewise  a steelix isn't fully metallic instead  exposure to its natural environment  transforms its scales into shiny  metallic looking armor this process  almost reminds me of a scaly foot snail  a type of marine gastropod that  incorporates iron sulfide into  protective scales on its body almost  like a natural suit of medieval armor it  could see stelix perhaps undergoing a  similar process to form its own  protective scales in holding onto dear  life at the end of these creatures tales  you can perhaps spy a sable eye  reimagine not as a ghost but as a tiny  primate with strange eyes similar to  atazie palmer describes this species as  attracted to shiny objects which  unfortunately for this sable eye  included the metallic scales of a stelix  under the waves the aquatic leviathan  gyrados takes on the role of apex  predator in almost any aquatic ecosystem  they inhabit this creature recalls the  appearance of a deep sea or fish which  can grow up to 26 feet or 8 meters in  length smaller than this version of  gyrados but still pretty incredible  palmer imagines the marine biologists of  this world for decades didn't realize  the comparatively tiny magikarp were  early forms of gyrados which makes sense  in nearby a greater underwater ecosystem  flourishes including cloister and  aurelie camp all looking closer to the  real world animal influences  returning to legendaries we have the  awe-inspiring pokemon zapdos an enormous  bird with an incredible electric  discharge instead of being an elemental  deity this version of zapdos has an  extreme capacity for electricity  retention seeking out thunderstorms high  in the atmosphere and gathering  electricity like a living lightning rod  a very interesting reimagining another  electric pokemon is raichu the evolution  of pikachu that also looks quite  rodent-like as you might have already  spotted this image also contains voltorb  reimagined as a pokeball mimiking  armadillo and magnemites and magnatons  as magnetic crabs with red and  blue-colored claws in my opinion truly  hilarious designs for some of the most  outside the box and challenging original  pokemon another ancient and seldom scene  legendary is lugia redesigned as a  lifeform with more streamlined aquatic  features this guardian of the seas looks  like something from the pleisiasoria  order just even larger  near lugia are some additional  underwater species like quillfish and  sidra all of which have far more detail  than in their original designs  in the skies the immense legendary  yveltal redesigned here as an avian  super predator casts a dark shadow over  most flying types whenever it comes out  of its deep hibernation in designing  this ancient creature palmer took  inspiration from the late jurassic  feather dinosaur ichi an animal whose  wings are perhaps the closest thing  nature has ever produced to a dragons  swooping just out of reach of yuvitol's  claws are latios and latias with this  duo palmer has merged the stylized  airplane-like elements of the original  designs with the anatomy of various  real-life bird species creating more  plausible but still agile looking life  forms and in the other corner of this  image you can spot a scalier-looking  salamance whose wings according to  palmer are formed from membranes  extended over an enlarged set of ribs  this might seem far-fetched but  real-life draco lizards are capable of  gliding flight thanks to a set of wings  that are indeed supported by their  elongated ribs as you can see by this  skeleton  and since we touched on groudon in part  one we have to touch on its oceanic  counterpoint kyogre which looks  downright awe-inspiring in realistic  detail homer imagines this legendary as  a leviathan somewhere between a whale  and the absolute largest of marine  reptiles to capture prey kyogre can use  a dazzling bioluminescent display to  lure in smaller species like tentacruel  above the feeding kyogre a swarm of  frillish and jelly scent drift by their  anatomy much closer to that of an earth  jellyfish and if you look closely you  might have spotted sharpedo whose  stylized body has been reworked with a  design that takes inspiration from  sharks of course but also ocean sunfish  a strange and truncated looking organism  this sharpedo is going up against the  nautilus-like amistar another  prehistoric species that was recently  revived and as our grand finale we have  the mythical and legendary rayquaza  looking truly epic in realistic detail  this mighty serpent is exhibiting an  unknown display of bioluminescence as it  soars through the upper atmosphere a  site that would be intimidating to  behold exactly how such a creature is  capable of flight is unknown and would  likely require a variety of specialized  flight organs believe it or not there is  a type of snake called a paradise tree  snake that can glide for brief periods  by leaping off trees holding its body  flat and curving in a way which allows  it to coast through the air rayquaza of  course is taking this to a new level and  there's an entire aviary of flying types  flocking in rayquaza's shadow including  pidgeot braviary and others all of whom  look much more like birds you might see  here on earth  and with that we've covered the majority  of realistic pokemon in the project if  you want to dive even deeper into this  world there are extra designs including  some additional legendaries on rj  palmer's site and in his art books so  check the links below if you're  interested for now this is the final  part of the series but if you enjoyed  this entry please lend your support and  like subscribe and hit the notification  icon to stay up to date on all things  curious see you in the next video

